outputs:
{"answer":"According to the EU AI Act excerpts provided, providers of general-purpose AI models face the following fines:

The Commission may impose fines not exceeding **3% of their annual total worldwide turnover in the preceding financial year or EUR 15,000,000, whichever is higher** (Article 101(1)).

These fines may be imposed when the Commission finds that the provider intentionally or negligently:

(a) infringed the relevant provisions of the Regulation (Article 101(1)(a));
(b) failed to comply with a request for a document or information, or supplied incorrect, incomplete or misleading information (Article 101(1)(b));
(c) failed to comply with a measure requested by the Commission (Article 101(1)(c));
(d) failed to make available access to the general-purpose AI model for evaluation purposes (Article 101(1)(d)).

When fixing the fine amount, the Commission must consider "the nature, gravity and duration of the infringement, taking due account of the principles of proportionality and appropriateness" (Article 101(1)). The fines imposed must be "effective, proportionate and dissuasive" (Article 101(3)).

Additionally, the Court of Justice of the European Union has unlimited jurisdiction to review these decisions and may "cancel, reduce or increase the fine imposed" (Article 101(5)).","citations":["Article 101(1-6)","Recitals 169-172","Recitals 162-163","Recitals 98-101","Recital 164"],"error":null,"refused":false}

